# Cloudflare Pages rewrites for the anonymous link surface.
#
# `expo export -p web --output static` emits dynamic routes as literal files
# named after the parameter, so a real token URL is a 404 until the host
# rewrites to one. 200 = rewrite (serve that file, keep the URL), not a
# redirect — the client router then reads the real token off location.pathname.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Pages takes the first matching rule, so deeper routes come
# first: with only the catch-all at the bottom, `/s/<token>/references` and
# `/s/<token>/crew/<id>` would both be served the shoot view. That is a silently
# wrong page, not an error, which is why the order is asserted in the export
# check rather than trusted.
#
# Every route added under `/s/` needs a rule here. The export emits one file per
# dynamic route, and a route with no rule is a 404 for every real URL.
#
# UNVERIFIED against a live Cloudflare Pages deployment. The bracket characters
# in the targets are still the part most likely to need percent-encoding
# (%5B/%5D) or a different form (S-2 F-3), and there are now two of them. If it
# resists, the proven fallback is `"output": "single"` in app.json, which needs
# no rewrite at all.
/s/:token/crew/:crewId /s/[token]/crew/[crewId].html 200
/s/:token/references /s/[token]/references.html 200
/s/:token /s/[token].html 200
